        <!-- Closing CTA -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            <strong class="text-white">Running on a treadmill is not bad for you; for most people it is
            one of the safest places to run.</strong> The real downsides are an identical stride,
            heat, falls from a moving belt, adding mileage too fast and a pace the machine will
            not ease off — and each has a simple fix.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Vary speed and incline, put a fan on, clip the safety key on, build up gradually and slow
            down when you need to. The myths about ruined form and wrecked knees do not hold up,
            but anyone with a heart, lung or balance condition should check with a doctor first.
            For the full set of habits that prevent falls, see our
            <a href="/treadmill-safety-tips/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">treadmill safety tips</a>.
          </p>
